@Bradley 
Martin is right, but you're not wrong. Oslofjord is certainly a fjord in ordinary geographic usage, and Oslo sits at its inner end. But you have a point about the geology: Oslofjord isn't a classic glacially carved fjord in the strict sense; it follows an ancient fault/rift valley that was later shaped by glaciation.
So the crossword clue is fair, as it's, “Capital with fjord tours,” not “Capital adjoining a textbook example of a glacially excavated U-shaped marine inlet.”

@Times Rita

I had a woman who cleaned my house for 16 years. Not only did she clean my house, she taught ME how to clean my house. Her name is Patty, and they day before she came, we all had to do what we called "Patty-proofing." She had to leave when her husband experienced some ... let's just say issues, and she needed to divorce him (he gave away all their $$ including children's" and we helped her. She's doing much better now,  I'm happy to say. 

Anyway, we still refer to cleaning before the cleaners as "Patty-proofing." And before company comes... "Patty-proofing." And during times when we had no cleaners, we Patty-proofed because we needed to. Patty is, was, and will always be the best cleaner ever, and the kindest person ever. We learn so much from people who touch our lives.

